<section id="part-a-2"><h3>A.2: Recover Homographies</h3>
<p>I selected corresponding points with the <a href="https://cal-cs180.github.io/fa23/hw/proj3/tool.html">correspondence tool</a> and saved their (x, y) coordinates in JSON files. I applied the photos’ EXIF orientation when loading them so the arrays matched the upright images used for point selection.</p>
<p>A homography maps a source point (x, y) to a destination point (x′, y′). I fix the bottom-right entry at 1, which leaves eight unknown entries:</p>
<pre aria-label="Homography equation"><code>    [x′]   [h₁ h₂ h₃] [x]
  w [y′] = [h₄ h₅ h₆] [y]
    [ 1]   [h₇ h₈  1] [1]

w = h₇x + h₈y + 1</code></pre>
<p>Substituting w into the first two rows gives two linear equations per point pair:</p>
<pre aria-label="Two rows of the homography linear system"><code>[x y 1 0 0 0 −xx′ −yx′]             [x′]
[0 0 0 x y 1 −xy′ −yy′] h =         [y′]

h = [h₁ h₂ h₃ h₄ h₅ h₆ h₇ h₈]ᵀ</code></pre>
<p>I stack these rows into Ah = b. With n pairs, A has 2n rows and eight columns. More than four pairs give an overdetermined system, which I solve with <code>np.linalg.lstsq</code>.</p>
