<section id="part-a-3"><h3>A.3: Warp the Images</h3>
<p>I transform the four image corners with H, round the bounds outward, and create an output pixel grid. For each output pixel, I apply H⁻¹ and divide by the homogeneous coordinate to find its position in the source image. This inverse mapping avoids holes between forward-mapped source pixels. I keep a separate validity mask for pixels whose source coordinates fall inside the image.</p>
<p>Nearest-neighbor interpolation rounds each valid source coordinate and copies that pixel. Bilinear interpolation uses the fractional distances dx and dy to weight the four neighboring pixels:</p>
<pre aria-label="Bilinear interpolation equation"><code>I(x, y) = (1 − dx)(1 − dy) I(x₀, y₀)
        + dx(1 − dy) I(x₁, y₀)
        + (1 − dx)dy I(x₀, y₁)
        + dx dy I(x₁, y₁)</code></pre>
<p>I implemented both interpolation methods with NumPy. At the last row or column, I clamp the upper neighbor to the image boundary. Invalid output pixels remain black and have a false mask value.</p>
<p>For rectification, I selected four object corners with Matplotlib’s <code>ginput</code> in clockwise order and mapped them to a rectangle defined by hand. These are the two original photos:</p>
