<section id="part-a-4"><h3>A.4: Blend the Images Into a Mosaic</h3>
<p>For each pair, I warp the left photo into the right photo’s coordinate system with bilinear interpolation. I compute a shared canvas that contains both images, then use the warped image’s offset to place it and its validity mask. The right image stays unwarped.</p>
<p>I use the distance transform of each mask to set blending weights. Each mask has a one-pixel false border before the distance calculation, so the canvas edges count as image boundaries. A pixel farther from an image boundary receives more weight. If only one image covers a pixel, that image receives full weight.</p>
<pre aria-label="Distance-weighted blending equation"><code>w_left  = d_left  / (d_left + d_right)
w_right = d_right / (d_left + d_right)

mosaic = w_left · left + w_right · right</code></pre>
<p>Where both distances are zero, I leave the output black. The masks record valid image coverage separately from pixel color, so valid black pixels still contribute to the blend.</p>
<h4>Bedroom Mosaic</h4>
<figure><a href="/images/cs180/project-4/a/bedroom_mosaic.jpg"><img src="/images/cs180/project-4/a/bedroom_mosaic.jpg" width="1779" height="2000" loading="lazy" alt="Bedroom mosaic with distance-weighted blending."></a><figcaption>Bedroom Mosaic with Distance-Weighted Blending</figcaption></figure>
<p>The wall posters align closely. The headband and stuffed animals have visible doubling, consistent with the depth differences that affected the original 12-point fit. Feathering softens the transition but cannot align those objects with the wall homography.</p>
<h4>Kitchen Mosaic</h4>
<figure><a href="/images/cs180/project-4/a/kitchen_mosaic.jpg"><img src="/images/cs180/project-4/a/kitchen_mosaic.jpg" width="2000" height="1995" loading="lazy" alt="Kitchen mosaic with distance-weighted blending."></a><figcaption>Kitchen Mosaic with Distance-Weighted Blending</figcaption></figure>
<p>The fridge details align closely. The containers above the fridge show doubling, and there is a visible brightness difference near the cabinet transition. The homography was fitted to points on the fridge front.</p>
<h4>Outdoors Mosaic</h4>
<figure><a href="/images/cs180/project-4/a/outdoors_mosaic.jpg"><img src="/images/cs180/project-4/a/outdoors_mosaic.jpg" width="2000" height="1879" loading="lazy" alt="Outdoors mosaic with distance-weighted blending."></a><figcaption>Outdoors Mosaic with Distance-Weighted Blending</figcaption></figure>
<p>The buildings join reasonably at this display size, but the left side stretches strongly. The selected points occupy a small part of the overlap, so the low error at those points does not establish a good fit across the full image. Foreground trees also have a different depth from the buildings.</p>
